"**Shakespeare plays that explicitly name or allude to *pear* fruit**\n\n| Play | Passage (including the word \u201cpear\u201d) | Context of the reference |\n|------|-------------------------------------|--------------------------|\n| **All\u2019s Well That Ends Well** | \u201cAnd your virginity, your old virginity, **is like one of our French withered pears**: it looks ill, it eats drily; marry, \u2019tis a withered pear.\u201d (Act\u202fI, Scene\u202f1) | Parolles (the court jester) uses the phrase \u201cFrench withered pears\u201d as a bawdy simile to compare Helena\u2019s virginity to a fruit that has gone bad. The remark carries a sexual innuendo that was likely understood by the audiences of the time. |\n| **Romeo and Juliet** | \u201c... **An open\u2011arse, thou a popp'rin pear**.\u201d (Act\u202fII, Scene\u202f1) | Mercutio coins the phrase \u201cpoppy\u2011rin pear\u201d (a regional name for the *medlar* or a pear found near Ypres) as a bawdy, profane insult toward Romeo. The line is part of Mercutio\u2019s quick\u2011wit banter and is an example of Shakespeare\u2019s playful use of the word *pear* for comic effect. |\n\n### How we found them\n\n1. **All\u2019s Well That Ends Well** \u2013 Through a dedicated search for \u201cFrench withered pears\u201d and by opening the play in the (free) Open\u2011Source Shakespeare archive.  \n   *Source excerpt*: `https://henripeyrefi.ws.gc.cuny.edu/2018/02/28/shakespeares-french-pears-and-claret\u2026`\n\n2. **Romeo and Juliet** \u2013 By searching for \u201cpopp'rin pear\u201d and by reading the relevant passage in the Open\u2011Source Shakespeare version of the play.  \n   *Source excerpt*: `https://shakesyear.wordpress.com/2011/06/20/romeo-and-juliet-mercutios-misogyny-part-1-of-2/`\n\nNo other Shakespeare plays that are part of the standard Shakespearean canon contain the word *pear* (or direct allusions to pears) in their text according to contemporary searchable editions and scholarly discussions. If you come across a reference from a lesser\u2011known or disputed play, feel free to let me know!"